        <h2>How to talk to your doctor about radiation oncology</h2>
        <p>If your doctor recommends radiation therapy to treat cancer, you may have many questions. Our team will meet with you to explain what treatment involves and how it could affect you now and in the future.</p>
        <p>We encourage you to bring up any questions or concerns about your care or treatment options. Our nurses and support professionals are available to answer questions about what to expect and possible treatment side effects.</p>

        <h2>Radiation oncology: What to expect</h2>
        <p>Not all cancers react the same way to radiation treatments. Your doctor may recommend radiation therapy before, after or instead of surgery or chemotherapy. Multiple cancer experts consider the details of your case before recommending a plan that's right for you.</p>
        <p>Our radiation oncologists stay up to date with leading research to protect your health today and for years to come. We have the largest brachytherapy program to support Central and South Texas, and maintain a portfolio of clinical trials to offer the newest treatment regimens to our patients.</p>

        <h2>Types of radiation therapy we offer</h2>
        <p>You can access leading advances in radiation therapy at Mays Cancer Center. Our services include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>3-D conformal radiation therapy:</strong> Uses imaging technology to treat a tumor while avoiding healthy tissues.</li>
          <li><strong>Intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT):</strong> Shapes radiation to a tumor by breaking large radiation beams into smaller beams.</li>
          <li><strong>Image-guided radiation therapy (IGRT):</strong> Uses advanced imaging and mapping systems to account for small movements, such as breathing.</li>
          <li><strong>Stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT):</strong> Delivers very high radiation doses to a specific area with extreme precision, reducing treatment time.</li>
          <li><strong>Stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS):</strong> Treats brain tumors with five or fewer treatments, without a single incision.</li>
          <li><strong>Brachytherapy:</strong> Uses catheters or tiny devices placed within the body to deliver radiation close to a tumor.</li>
          <li><strong>Pediatric radiation therapy:</strong> Uses multiple technologies to treat cancer in children from infancy to young adulthood.</li>
          <li><strong>Pluvicto:</strong> A targeted radiation therapy used to treat advanced prostate cancer.</li>
          <li><strong>Transarterial radioembolization (TARE):</strong> A radiation treatment for liver cancer that delivers microscopic radioactive beads through the bloodstream.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Is radiation oncology right for me?</h2>
        <p>Your doctor may recommend radiation therapy depending on your cancer type, where the cancer is located, whether it has spread and your overall treatment plan. Radiation therapy may be used before surgery, after surgery, instead of surgery or along with chemotherapy.</p>
        <p>If radiation therapy may be part of your care, our team can help you understand your options, possible side effects and what the treatment schedule may look like.</p>

        <h2>Cancer research and clinical trials</h2>
        <p>Through our internationally recognized research program, we participate in and lead many national and local clinical trials. If eligible, you may be able to receive promising new therapies before they are widely available.</p>
        <p>For example, some radiation oncology studies explore how to deliver the same treatment effect in less time. Other research offers new hope for cancers that were once thought incurable.</p>

